WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.400
Industrial protocols are the communication rules that connect controllers, HMIs, sensors,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.000
actuators, historians, engineering tools, and supervisory systems.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.730
Their messages carry operational data and, in many cases,

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.000
actions that can affect physical processes.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Common patterns include polling, command and response, publish and subscribe,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000
controller coordination, historian collection, supervisory control,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.235
and privileged engineering access.

00:00:23.235 --> 00:00:26.895
Defenders can use these stable relationships to establish baselines and identify

00:00:26.895 --> 00:00:28.000
meaningful change.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.895
Modbus offers simple reads and writes but often relies on external controls for trust.

00:00:31.895 --> 00:00:37.000
DNP3 supports distributed telemetry and control, including efficient event reporting.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.965
OPC technologies enable interoperable data exchange,

00:00:39.965 --> 00:00:44.245
while OPC UA adds modern security capabilities that still require sound certificate and

00:00:44.245 --> 00:00:46.250
configuration management.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:50.660
EtherNet/IP and PROFINET operate within engineered vendor ecosystems where timing,

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.000
supported configurations, and device roles matter.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.145
Across every protocol, technical interpretation must include process state, architecture,

00:00:57.145 --> 00:00:58.950
user authority, and planned maintenance.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.895
Good protection combines passive protocol-aware monitoring, segmentation,

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:07.000
controlled engineering and remote access, secure configuration, change management,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.000
and coordinated incident response.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.270
Many industrial protocols were designed for reliability before current cybersecurity

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:14.250
expectations.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:18.000
Effective security respects that history without accepting unmanaged risk,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.000
bringing security, operations, and engineering together around evidence and safe action.
